  "difficulty_level": "H2",
  "difficulty_reason": "The proof rules out a dense image by a polynomial-trace argument in which the density of finite-order traces 2cos(k pi/n) converts any dense image into an arbitrarily small unfaithful perturbation, so discreteness of the image is forced by robust faithfulness alone. It then passes to PSL2(R), uses the geometrically finite structure of the hyperbolic surface, and eliminates parabolics in two genus cases: for genus zero by perturbing a cusp generator to finite order, and for genus greater than zero by writing the cusp word as a product of commutators and simple loops and applying the retained commutator-submersion lemma to realise the perturbation while deforming only two generators. The conclusion that every element is hyperbolic then yields Anosov, equivalently quasi-isometric, in rank one. This deformation-and-surface-structure combination is research-level and clearly beyond ordinary PhD qualifying examinations in Lie theory or hyperbolic geometry.",
